// Table.GenerateByPage that skips empty pages and stays lazy.
//
// getNextPage receives the previous page (null on the first call) and returns the next
// page as a table, or null to stop. Carry the next cursor or link in the page's
// metadata. Every page, empty ones included, must have the declared table type: the
// output columns come from the first page, so an untyped empty first page
// (Table.FromRecords({})) loses every column.
//
// Microsoft's sample versions (TripPin 5, the GitHub sample, and the .pqm from TripPin 7
// on, also shown on Learn's helper-functions page) expand every page. On the Power
// Query engine that turns each empty page into a row of nulls, and the .pqm's
// empty-first-page guard drops every later page. This version filters empty pages out
// before expanding. Checked with PQTest 2.160.2:
// - generatebypage-check.query.pq next to this file: right rows in every case;
// - with an HTTP mock of 4 pages, Table.FirstN(t, 1) and Table.Schema(t) request one
//   page, like Microsoft's version. Table.Combine(listOfPages) also gives the right
//   rows but requests every page for both, so a navigator preview downloads the table.
(getNextPage as function) as table =>
    let
        listOfPages = List.Generate(
            () => getNextPage(null),
            (lastPage) => lastPage <> null,
            (lastPage) => getNextPage(lastPage)
        ),
        tableOfPages = Table.FromList(listOfPages, Splitter.SplitByNothing(), {"Column1"}),
        firstRow = tableOfPages{0}?,
        nonEmptyPages = Table.SelectRows(tableOfPages, each not Table.IsEmpty([Column1]))
    in
        if firstRow = null then
            Table.FromRows({})
        else
            Value.ReplaceType(
                Table.ExpandTableColumn(nonEmptyPages, "Column1", Table.ColumnNames(firstRow[Column1])),
                Value.Type(firstRow[Column1])
            )
